#### 5.6.4 \(\Upsilon\) efficiencies

Similarly to the single electron efficiency, the \(\Upsilon\) reconstruction efficiency is calculated by applying the same set of cuts to the \(e^{+}e^{-}\) from \(\Upsilon\) decays. These efficiencies are calculated separately for \(\Upsilon(1S)\), \(\Upsilon(2S)\) and \(\Upsilon(3S)\) states as shown in Figures 5.13, 5.14, 5.15 respectively. Again, the uncertainties are calculated with the binomial distribution [162].

The \(p_{T}\) dependence and \(y\) dependence of the efficiency is investigated. As expected, the efficiency is larger for \(|y|<0.5\) than \(0.5<|y|<1.0\) due to limited acceptance effects. That is, electrons from \(\Upsilon\) decays in \(0.5<|y|<1.0\) have large \(p_{z}\), thus being more likely to fall out of acceptance of the TPC than those with \(p_{z}\approx 0\) for \(\Upsilon\) with \(|y|<0.5\). Also, the efficiency increases as the \(\Upsilon\)\(p_{T}\) increases.

The above efficiencies are used to correct the reconstructed \(\Upsilon\) signal.

