#### Addition smearing of electron \(p_{T}\)

The embeddings in STAR result in too narrow lineshapes of \(\Upsilon\) and \(J/\psi\) compared to the observed signal in the data. This has to be corrected by adding a component to smear the electron momentum resolution. It is done by using a Gaussian smearing of electron \(p_{T}\). A \(p_{T}^{random}\) is generated from a Gaussian, with a width set to \(\frac{\sigma(p_{T})}{p_{T}}=a\cdot p_{T}\). This is then added to the reconstructed momentum \(\vec{p}^{REC}\) to the \(\vec{p}^{mean}\) using Equation 5.6.

\[\left\{\begin{array}{l}\left.\begin{array}{l}p_{x}^{emear}=p_{x}^{REC}+p_{ T}^{random}\cdot p_{T}^{MC}\cdot\cos(\phi^{MC})\\ p_{y}^{emear}=p_{y}^{REC}+p_{T}^{random}\cdot p_{T}^{MC}\cdot\sin(\phi^{MC}) \\ p_{z}^{smear}=p_{z}^{REC}+p_{T}^{random}\cdot p_{T}^{MC}\cdot\sinh(\eta^{MC}) \end{array}\right\}\right. \tag{5.6}\]

Where \(p_{T}^{MC}\) is the \(p_{T}\) of the Monte Carlo track and \(\eta^{MC}\), \(\phi^{MC}\) are \(\eta\) and \(\phi\) of the Monte Carlo track. The \(a\) parameter is determined by performing a \(\chi^{2}\) scan in search for the best fit between smeared \(J/\psi\) lineshape from embedding and data [166, 167]. \(J/\psi\) is used over \(\Upsilon\)

