After applying all the above cuts, which are listed in the Table 6.1, the number of events is \(77\ \mathrm{M}\). This is because of low VPD efficiency and vertex quality in Run09. Basic event distributions, like \(V_{z}\) and \(refMult\) are shown in Figure 6.1a and Figure 6.1b respectively.

### 6.3 Track selection

In \(J/\psi\) analysis, track selection is done in similar way as for \(\Upsilon\) analysis. Track quality cuts are described in Subsection 6.3.1, electron identification cuts are described in Subsection 6.3.2 and kinematic cuts are described in Subsection 6.3.3.

#### Track quality cuts

Track quality for \(J/\psi\) studies is ensured, by requiring charge \(|q|=1\), \(|\eta|<1.0\) and \(0<flag<1000\). By selecting tracks with \(nFitPts>15\), a good momentum resolution is provided, while \(nFitPts/nFitPtsMax>0.52\) is applied to avoid track splitting. In addition \(DCA<3\mathrm{cm}\) cut is used to select primary tracks with a high probability of originating from the primary vertex. The track quality cuts are Summarized in Table 6.2.

#### Electron identification cuts

Electron identification cuts for \(J/\psi\) reconstruction are selected first with TPC \(\frac{dE}{dx}\) by applying \(-1<n\sigma_{e}<2\) cut. The distribution of \(\frac{dE}{dx}\) vs. \(p\) measured by the TPC is shown in Figure 6.2a [178]. In addition TOF is used for hadron rejection at low momentum below

Figure 6.1: Figure 6.1a: Primary vertex \(V_{z}\) distribution after cuts. A Gaussian fit is shown as red line. Figure 6.1b: Reference multiplicity \(refMult\) distribution for accepted events.

\(p<1.4\mathrm{GeV/c}\). Kaons and protons are well separated from electrons in inverse particle velocity (in the units of speed of light) \(\beta^{-1}=\frac{c}{v}\) as can be seen in Figure 6.3[178] up to \(p\approx 1.4\mathrm{\,GeV/c}\). This yields a higher purity electron sample as can be seen for example in Figure 6.2b.

For momenta \(p>2.0\mathrm{\,GeV/c}\), as hadron bands start to overlap with electrons in \(\frac{dE}{dx}\) due to relativistic rise, the BEMC is used. Tracks are matched to BEMC towers with tower energy \(E>0.1\mathrm{\,GeV}\) to avoid noise. Finally, hadrons are rejected by a tower energy to track momentum ratio \(E/p>0.5\) cut.

Electron identification cuts are summarized in Table 6.3.

#### Kinematic cuts

Kinematic cuts are applied in order to reduce background. A \(p_{T}>0.8\mathrm{\,GeV/c}\) cut, rejects low momentum tracks, which have low probability of originating from \(J/\psi\) decays. For example, a \(J/\psi\) with \(p_{T}\approx 0\mathrm{\,GeV/c}\), will decay into 2 back-to-back electrons, each with \(p\approx 1.5\mathrm{\,GeV/c}\).

