### 6.6 Systematic uncertainties

#### TOF matching efficiency uncertainty

TOF matching efficiency is calculated by using a scaled hadron efficiency as described in Section 6.5.2. It is compared with the upper and lower uncertainty limit on the efficiency and with the efficiency calculated from electron sample. The maximum deviation is chosen as an uncertainty, which affects the results by \(1\%\) to \(3\%\).

#### 6.6.2 \(\beta^{-1}\) cut efficiency systematic uncertainty

The \(\beta^{-1}\) cut efficiency is calculated both using photonic electrons and a Gaussian fit to the \(\beta^{-1}\) distribution. The difference is included as a systematic uncertainty and it influences the \(J/\psi\) cross section by \(-4\%\) to \(3\%\).

#### Additional smearing uncertainty

The uncertainty in the additional smearing parameter \(A=0.71\pm 0.05\%\) affects the results by \(\pm 1\%\).

Figure 6.17: \(J/\psi\) yield vs. \(p_{T}\) for VPD-MB and BHT1 data [180]. It is fitted with a power law (red) and Tsallis q-exponential (blue) functions.

#### TPC tracking efficiency

Tracking efficiency uncertainty is calculated by changing \(nFitPts\pm 2\). The effect is \(-4\%\) to \(2\%\).

#### Shape of \(J/\psi\)\(p_{T}\) and rapidity spectra

The influence of the shape of \(J/\psi\)\(p_{T}\) and rapidity spectra on \(J/\psi\) reconstruction efficiency in embedding is studied by comparing realistic (\(p_{T}\) and \(y\) weighed) and flat distributions. The results change by \(-8\%\) and \(5\%\) for \(p_{T}\) and rapidity spectra respectively.

#### \(J/\psi\) spin alignment

The influence of \(J/\psi\) spin alignment is investigated by assuming a \(\lambda\) parameter in Equation 5.9 from PHENIX [182] results. The effect is \(-3\%\).

#### \(n\sigma_{e}\) efficiency uncertainty

To check the influence of constraints in the \(n\sigma_{e}\) cut efficiency estimation, the fit range of photonic electrons is changed from \(|n\sigma_{e}|<3\) to \(|n\sigma_{e}|<4\). In addition, the constraints are varied by \(5\sigma\), because the changes are very small. All these changes affect the results by \(-8\%\) to \(5\%\).

#### TPC resolution effect on BEMC matching

The BEMC matching efficiency is calculated by projecting reconstructed and MC tracks to BEMC hits. The difference is taken as a systematic uncertainty and affects the results by \(2\%\).

#### \(\frac{E}{p}\) cut efficiency systematic uncertainty

The uncertainty associated with the \(\frac{E}{p}\) cut efficiency calculation is investigated by comparing efficiency from embedding and photonic electrons. The effect is estimated to be \(-9\%\) to \(-7\%\).

#### Bin width correction uncertainty

The systematic uncertainty connected to the bin width correction is estimated by changing the fit function used to correct the data. The difference between using a power law function inEquation 6.4 and Tsallis q-exponential from Equation 6.5 is treated as an uncertainty. This affects the results by \(-3\%\) to \(42\%\).

#### Signal extraction method

The signal extraction method uncertainty is calculated by comparing signal obtained from histogram counts and fitted lineshape. It is found to be \(+35\%\) for \(0<p_{T}<2\,\mathrm{GeV}/\mathrm{c}\) to \(+50\%\) for \(2<p_{T}<4\;\mathrm{GeV}/\mathrm{c}\).

#### Invariant mass cut uncertainty

In order to estimate the systematic uncertainty related to invariant mass cut, the \(2.7<m_{ee}<3.2\;\mathrm{GeV}/\mathrm{c}^{2}\) cut is changed to \(2.9<m_{ee}<3.2\;\mathrm{GeV}/\mathrm{c}^{2}\). The effect is found to range from \(+16\%\) to \(+20\%\).

#### Uncertainty related to radiative decays

Radiative decays \(J/\psi\to e^{+}e^{-}\gamma\) constitute a fraction of \(J/\psi\to e^{+}e^{-}\) decays [183]. The energy carried off by \(\gamma\) is missing when reconstructing \(J/\psi\) signal using dielectrons. This causes the reconstruction of smaller \(m_{ee}\) than \(m_{J/\psi}\), thus contributing to the tail in the signal. This effect is not included in STAR simulations, so a systematic uncertainty was estimated using the model in Reference [183] and was found to be \(4\%\)[180, 184] for the \(2.7<m_{ee}<3.2\;\mathrm{GeV}/\mathrm{c}^{2}\) cut.

#### Uncertainty on integrated luminosity

Similarly to \(\Upsilon\) studies in Section 5.7.8, the uncertainty is \(\pm 8\%\).

