The results for \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\), \(\Upsilon(1S)\) and \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) are shown in the Figure 7.2. The data are compared to CEM [197, 70] and Color Glass Condensate Non-relativistic QCD (CGC+NRQCD) model calculation [198, 199, 66]. The \(\Upsilon(1S)\) yield is consistent with CEM model and \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\) is consistent for \(p_{T}>4\rm{GeV/c}\), while \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) is overestimated by the model, except for \(p_{T}>6\rm{~{}GeV/c}\). In the case of CGC+NRQCD prediction, the \(\Upsilon(1S)\) is consistent with the data for \(2<p_{T}<8\rm{~{}GeV/c}\), while \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\) and \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) is overestimated. It is important to note, that authors of the model warned [199], that additional corrections are needed in order to describe the cross section at low-\(p_{T}\). This is however, first such calculation of CGC+NRQCD for \(\Upsilon\). Due to the fact that a NLO+NRQCD calculations require \(p_{T}\gg m_{\Upsilon}\), they are not available for the \(p_{T}<10\rm{~{}GeV/c}\).

These results were shown during Quark Matter 2017 as STAR Preliminary [196], but have since been updated with additional systematic uncertainties.

