#### Rapidity spectra

A rapidity distribution is obtained in a similar way. The cross section for the \(1<y<-0.5\) and \(0.5<y<1\) is added together in \(0.5<|y|<1\) and reflected symmetrically. The rapidity dependence for \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\), \(\Upsilon(1S)\) and \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) is presented in Figure 7.3.

A dip at mid-rapidity (\(|y|<0.5\)) is observed in the \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) cross section, which also contributes to a dip in \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\). This is unexpected, however a smaller yield of \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) signal is observed already in Figure 5.7a. It may be a downward fluctuation in the \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) signal or a physical effect. Given the higher \(\Upsilon\) reconstruction efficiency at mid-rapidity compared to \(0.5<|y|<1\), the difference is amplified during efficiency correction.

The data are compared to the CEM [70, 197] and CGC+NRQCD [66, 198, 199] models in Figure 7.3a and Figure 7.3b respectively. CEM calculation for \(\Upsilon(1S)\) is consistent with the STAR results. The rest of CEM predictions overestimates the data, however the

Figure 7.2: Invariant cross section of \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\) (red diamonds), \(\Upsilon(1S)\) (green diamonds) and \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) (blue diamonds) compared to CEM model calculation for \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\) (red checked band), \(\Upsilon(1S)\) (green band) and \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) (blue checked band) [70, 197]. The results are also compared to CGC+NRQCD predictions for \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\) (red shaded area), \(\Upsilon(1S)\) (green shaded area) and \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) (blue shaded area) [66, 198, 199].

cross section for \(0.5<|y|<1\) is within \(2\sigma\) of the model prediction. The CEM predictions for the \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\), which also contribute to the \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\) are in fact \(\Upsilon(1S)\) scaled by the respective ratios, which are constant. These ratios are determined at \(\sqrt{s}=7\,{\rm TeV}\) and are found to be \(\frac{\Upsilon(2S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}=0.51\) and \(\frac{\Upsilon(3S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}=0.35\)[197] including branching ratios [15]. These values are much larger than the world data suggest [82].

In the case of CGC+NRQCD, the data are overestimated, however it has to be noted, that additional corrections are needed to describe the data at low-\(p_{T}\)[199]. The excess observed for \(p_{T}<2\;{\rm GeV}/{\rm c}\) in Figure 7.2 has a large contribution to the integrated cross section and shifts the rapidity distribution to higher values.

The rapidity distribution is flatter, than the one observed by STAR at \(\sqrt{s}=200\;{\rm GeV}\)[151, 196], which is qualitatively confirmed by the trend predicted in the models.

#### Ratios

The data allowed extraction of \(\frac{\Upsilon(2S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}\), \(\frac{\Upsilon(3S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}\) and \(\frac{\Upsilon(2S+3S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}\) ratios. Each ratio is corrected for the mass cut efficiency and reconstruction efficiency. Figure 7.4 shows the STAR data compared to the world data [76, 82, 165, 186, 187, 190, 191, 195, 200, 201, 202]. STAR results are within \(\approx 2\sigma\) of the constant fits to the world data from Reference [82].

Figure 7.3: Figure 7.3a: Rapidity distribution of \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\) (red diamonds), \(\Upsilon(1S)\) (green diamonds) and \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) (blue diamonds). The data are compared to CEM model predictions for \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\) (red checked band), \(\Upsilon(1S)\) (green band) and \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) (blue checked band) [70, 197] respectively. Furthermore, the data are also compared in Figure 7.3b to CGC+NRQCD model for \(\Upsilon(1S+2S+3S)\) (red shaded area), \(\Upsilon(1S)\) (green shaded area) and \(\Upsilon(2S+3S)\) (blue shaded area) [66, 198, 199].

All the ratios shown are integrated in \(p_{T}\) and rapidity. A \(p_{T}\) dependence of ratios is observed at LHC [75, 76, 77, 165, 201, 202]. In fact the ratios increase with increasing \(p_{T}\), which gives a small contribution to the integrated ratios. The STAR data are more consistent with the values up to \(p_{T}\approx 6\,{\rm GeV/c}\). In addition, the smaller \(\mathchar 28935\relax(2S+3S)\) cross section observed at mid-rapidity in Figure 7.3 also affects the ratios.

The dependence of \(\mathchar 28935\relax\) ratios on charged particle multiplicity, represented by \(TofMult\) is shown in Figure 7.5. The \(\frac{\Upsilon(2S+3S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}\) and \(\frac{\Upsilon(2S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}\) ratios are below the world data average for \(TofMult<8\). However, the \(\frac{\Upsilon(2S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}\) for \(TofMult>8\) is consistent with the fit to the world data within uncertainties. A good agreement is found between world data fit and \(\frac{\Upsilon(3S)}{\Upsilon(1S)}\) ratio. Within available precision, there is no significant change of the rations as function of the charged hadron multiplicity. Thus we do not observe a significant modification of \(\Upsilon\) relative yields due to larger hadron density.

#### Event activity dependence

Using the fully corrected \(\Upsilon\) and event multiplicity (a measure of event activity) distributions a normalized \(\Upsilon\) yield \(\frac{N_{\Upsilon}}{\langle N_{\Upsilon}\rangle}\) as a function of normalized multiplicity is calculated with Equation 7.3:

\[\frac{N_{\Upsilon}}{\langle N_{\Upsilon}\rangle}=\frac{N_{ev}^{all}\;N_{ \Upsilon}^{lin}}{N_{ev}^{bin}\;N_{\Upsilon}^{all}} \tag{7.3}\]

Where \(N_{ev}^{all}\) is the number of all Min-Bias events and \(N_{ev}^{bin}\) is the number of Min-Bias in a given bin. Similarly, \(N_{\Upsilon}^{all}\) is the number of all \(\Upsilon\) and \(N_{\Upsilon}^{lin}\) is the number of \(\Upsilon\) in a bin. The points are placed according to the Equation 7.4:

\[\frac{N_{ch}}{\langle N_{ch}\rangle}=\frac{\langle N_{ch}\rangle^{bin}}{\langle N _{ch}\rangle^{all}} \tag{7.4}\]With the \(\left\langle N_{ch}\right\rangle^{bin}\) being the mean multiplicity in a bin of Min-Bias distribution and \(\left\langle N_{ch}\right\rangle^{bin}\) a mean of the entire Min-Bias distribution.

The normalized \(\Upsilon\) yield \(\frac{N_{T}}{\left\langle N_{T}\right\rangle}\) is plotted as a function of normalized multiplicity \(\frac{\left\langle N_{ch}\right\rangle^{bin}}{\left\langle N_{ch}\right\rangle ^{all}}\) and compared to world data in Figure 7.6 [1, 2, 4] and to the models in Figure 7.7 [5, 6, 172, 203]. The STAR \(\Upsilon\) data are consistent with the world data trend and \(J/\psi\) data, which exhibit a strong rise with normalized multiplicity. A \(p_{T}\) dependence is observed and is indicated in the shift of central value of the \(\Upsilon(1S)\) results for \(p_{T}>4\ \mathrm{GeV/c}\) with respect to \(p_{T}\)-integrated, but given the uncertainties it is consistent with no dependence.

The STAR \(\Upsilon\) are compared to PYTHIA8 calculation using STAR Heavy Flavor (HF) tune [172, 203], which exhibits a stronger rise for \(p_{T}>4\mathrm{GeV/c}\). Both calculations are consistent with the data and suggest influence of multiple parton interactions (MPI) on \(\Upsilon\) production. In addition, the String Percolation Model for \(J/\psi\)[5, 6] is compared to the data and a good agreement is found. The STAR \(\Upsilon\) data are fitted with a quadratic function \(f(x)=ax^{2}\), which is motivated by the String Percolation Model, and a \(1\sigma\) confidence band is plotted. The data are well described by the fits.

The STAR \(\Upsilon\) results suggest similar behavior for \(\Upsilon\) and \(J/\psi\). Furthermore, similar behavior is observed at RHIC and LHC energy for both. This gives a hint of collective effects, which may be present at high multiplicity in \(p+p\) collisions. Moreover, the behavior may be due to MPI as suggested by ALICE \(D\) meson results and PYTHIA8 [3].

#### 7.1.6 \(x_{t}\) dependence

In pQCD, an inclusive invariant cross section \(\sigma^{inv}\) is expected to follow a power law scaling according to Equation 7.5 [204].

\[\sigma^{inv}\equiv E\frac{d^{3}\sigma}{d^{3}p}=\frac{F(x_{T})}{p_{T}^{n(x_{T},\sqrt{s})}}=\frac{F^{\prime}(x_{T})}{\sqrt{s}^{n(x_{T},\sqrt{s})}} \tag{7.5}\]

Here, the \(F(x_{T})\) and \(F^{\prime}(x_{T})\) are functions describing the \(x_{T}\) spectra and \(x_{T}=\frac{2p_{T}}{\sqrt{s}}\). The \(n(x_{T},\sqrt{s})\) is the exponent of the power law decrease of the inclusive invariant cross section, which is equal to the number of partons participating in the process. For a \(2\to 2\) process in the parton model \(n=4\), as there are 4 partons taking active role in the process [205]. In general, the \(n>4\) if there are more partons participating in the process [206]. Furthermore, the running of \(\alpha_{s}\) and changes in the PDFs and FFs cause small changes in \(n(x_{T},\sqrt{s})\), which in turn cause small scaling violations.

